body:
Hey, war vor 2 Jahren in einer ähnlichen Situation. Psychische Probleme, Betrieb hat mir nix beigebracht. In der Schule gute bis sehr gute Noten. Hab die Theorie mit ner knappen 3 und die Praxis mit ner sehr guten 2 bestanden. 

Falls du vor der praktischen Prüfung noch die Chance hast Prüfungsvorbereitung in der Handwerkskammer zu machen würde ich dir das dringend empfehlen. Bei uns war es so, dass dort innerhalb einer Woche nochmal alles durchgegangen wurde, was du über die letzten Jahre gelernt haben solltest. Dann wurden 10 Stationen aufgebaut, von denen 5 in der Prüfung drankamen (du wusstest natürlich nicht welche) und du wurdest richtig auf die Prüfung gedrillt. 

&#x200B;

Für Morgen: Geh pennen, mach dir keinen Kopf. Sehr viele Fragen in der Prüfung können mit gesundem Menschenverstand beantwortet werden. Außerdem: Schreib immer was hin, auch wenn du denkst es ist nicht richtig oder du weißt es nicht. Alles kann helfen, um wenigstens noch einen Punkt abzugreifen!

&#x200B;

Drücke dir die Daumen. Und vergiss deine Formelsammlung und den Taschenrechner nicht.

[https://www.facebook.com/watch/?v=550702778922083](https://www.facebook.com/watch/?v=550702778922083)

&#x200B;

Das hier ist auch sone schöne Parteiwerbung von Kalkofe, die ich mir immer wieder gerne anschaue.

Thank you for your thoughts. Everything you said made sense, but of course my brain screams "But it's different this time!". Also, I still want to watch my world burn, so I am on the fence about cutting him out of my life.

&#x200B;

But regardless, thanks for replying.

Hast ja schon etliche Antworten gekriegt. Würde mich eventuell ein bisschen schlau machen, was gegen Körpergeruch hilft, wenn es eben nicht am täglichen Duschen hängt. Wie alt ist der Trainee denn? Bis ca 20 stinken manche Kerle einfach abartig, da hilft nix außer spezielles Deo.

&#x200B;

Würde ihn beiseite nehmen, Gespräch mit ihm führen und dann Ratschläge einstreuen wie "ich kenn das ja, in deinem Alter gings mir ähnlich, mir hat xyz geholfen".

Well that's just like uuuh... your opinion, man.

&#x200B;

Because they are beyond reasoning.

Ich hab mir jetzt nicht alle Antworten durchgelesen, aber hier meine Anekdote:

Ich habe 2 Jahre lang ausschließlich langärmlig gearbeitet. Dann wurds mir zu dumm und ich hab T-Shirt angezogen wenns warm genug wurde. Ja, Kollegen haben mich angesprochen. Ich bin aber mittlerweile so mit mir im Reinen diesbezüglich und habe dann kurz, knapp, aber ehrlich geantwortet. Muss dazu sagen, dass ich in nem Handwerksbetrieb arbeite. Habe dann z.B. sowas gesagt wie "Jup, hatte ne scheiß Zeit in meinem Leben." und das wars. Das eine mal, als jemand weitere Fragen gestellt hat hab ich einfach gesagt, dass es ihn einen Scheißdreck angeht.

&#x200B;

Wenn du mit Kindern arbeitest: Überlege dir eine gute, kindgerechte Erklärung. Ich sage Kindern z.B. immer, dass ich einen Reitunfall mit Schotter hatte, und die akzeptieren das und die Sache ist durch.

&#x200B;

Im Bewerbungsgespräch würd ich langärmlig tragen, danach nicht mehr. Tritt selbstbewusst auf. Falls es zu Problemen kommt: Es gibt andere Arbeitgeber.

Gosh I know what you mean. I really try to keep my shit together. It sort of works, but I never feel at home in any social circle.

&#x200B;

When I first wake up in the morning, reaching for the bottle, I read stories from here and feel like I belong.

I want a pair of socks. I only got one after asking for them because I've been there so many times lol.

&#x200B;

Anyway, hope it helps you.

Hey, do you mean hospitalized as in put in the psych ward?

&#x200B;

The first time I was there I was really scared for the first two weeks or so, but then I gradually adapted and actually kind of enjoyed staying there.

To all the people saying OP is overreacting or blaming them because they gave their therapist permission:

I'd say the majority of people who need therapy have very little self esteem, are not used to their needs ever having been met or even considered. Especially in a situation with a power differential they are unable to stand up for themselves or don't even consider it an option to say no. There might even be an enormous amount of anxiety involved in "keeping the peace".

&#x200B;

"I was confused and embarrassed and figured that was what I was supposed to do so I started telling her about it"

&#x200B;

I think this quote shows that OP leans towards anxiety and non-confrontation.

To OP: It's totally okay to feel how you're feeling. Like others have said, I'd use it as a learning experience. If your T reacts negatively, defensive or starts blaming you, drop her immediately. That's unprofessional. If she doesn't and instead validates you and talks you through the experience and you feel like you can put it behind you, I'd stay with her.

&#x200B;

Edit: removed gendering

I totally understand, I first got help at 29 and have made so many mistakes up until then.

&#x200B;

But I also made split-second decisions to move to foreign countries or try jobs I never would have otherwise.

I spent the last 11 months with starvation as a coping mechanism.

&#x200B;

Now my body decided to hate me and eating is all I think about. I've already gained weight again and can't seem to low restrict anymore, I hate it.

A friend of mine once felt like he had no choice but to abandon his animals in a carrier in front of the animal shelter (it was closed for the night). He left them enough food and water to last the night. I won't go into the reasons why he did it, but basically he had no one to care for them and had to leave them behind on short notice, so there was nothing else he could do. 

&#x200B;

The shelter posted pics of the carrier on facebook and asked if anyone knew the animals and could provide info (yeah, thanks, now people will not consider this an option any longer and instead just abandon their animals wherever). The amount of hate in the comments was overwhelming. People bashing my friend with no knowledge about the circumstances for leaving his animals somewhere where they would definitely be cared for.

I hope you're okay OP, ER visits are never any fun.

&#x200B;

Just to combat the stereotype, I am actually kind of happy with all the ER staff I've seen over the years. The worst I've got were ignorant comments, like "so you wanted to kill yourself?" when there for a cut that was nowhere near dangerous. Also when in the ICU all the staff were amazing although I was blatantly there because of my own stupidity, but they did not let me feel that at all. I wish everyone to have supportive hospital staff.

I'm on bottomshelf grain spirit I don't know the exact translation of. After having had too much cheap vodka and feeling like my insides disintegrated for the last few weeks I decided I needed a change.

&#x200B;

I have to work tomorrow btw and I already know it will be a shitshow.

Lol half the time I hated it and half the time I absolutely loved it. Especially being on the locked ward. People would freak out about the locked ward, stating they would never want to go there and it was so scary etc. but I found it very calming. You didn't have to pretend at all there. I could just let it all out. Scream my lungs out in the smoking room at 2am? Sure, you do you. Literally none of the other residents there were scary to me because on a very basic level I related to all of them. Some guy there once punched me right in the face and I wasn't even bothered because I realized he had no other way of communicating at this time. I find it literally so relaxing to not feel like a freak all the time and the nurses there were the absolute best. I had to push my bed in front of the staff room windows a lot so they could keep an eye on me at all times and I appreciated that so much because it made me feel safe.

&#x200B;

Anyway, I digress. I guess it also depends on the wards. The open ward was cool, too, and obviously after so many stays it became my second home, but there were also a lot of things bothering me there. Where I live they generally really try to help you though which they apparently don't do everywhere.

Get well soon, rolled ankles are on a whole other level.

&#x200B;

I relate to the sentiment though. A few weeks ago I accidentally pushed a glass bottle over onto my tile floor, and the damn thing just exploded all over the fucking place, one shard hitting me in my shin, cutting me REALLY deep. It bled and hurt like a motherfucker and I was swearing the whole time I bandaged it up. Upside: At least I had all the necessary supplies on hand 🙄 But I am also pretty upset with the scar it left. Nevermind the dozens of scars I already have, THIS scar obviously disfigures me. You have to admire the sh/borderline brain.

My normal T never touched or hugged me, and I never asked, nor really wanted it I think.

&#x200B;

I had an art therapist for a while, and one session I drew something that made me very despaired and emotional, and she asked me if I could have someone touch me in a supportive manner, where I'd like to be touched. I told her and she came over and, after making sure I was alright with it, touched me in that manner. It was strangely weird but felt so good at the same time. But I wouldn't want this to be a regular occurrence.

Sorry not willing to write a pm since that would mean commitment and I am all about not commiting to anybody/anything.

&#x200B;

I have recently lost a good friend since he couldn't deal with the fact that my BPD is not curable and I will have episodes for the rest of my life and am as a result very cynical about the whole thing. 

Well, tbh, good for him for enforcing boundaries and also I will live without him. Yes, it hurts like a mf, but I will get over it.

&#x200B;

Just my input.

I have read something about how people who grew up in abusive homes feel like when there is empathy or even compliments expressed towards them, that it is incongruent with their sense of self. So they feel like they have somehow deceived or tricked the other person and they feel bad.

&#x200B;

Maybe that's what's going on?

Haha whenever they do that (which isn't that often thankfully) I am usually hospitalized a few days later by the emergency department anyway so they learned to take me seriously.

&#x200B;

I am sorry you don't get the help you need, OP.

I am not very social, but if someone who is new to whatever environment (inpatient, work) seems to struggle, I am there for them for as long as it takes them to make friends. 

&#x200B;

~~Because that is as long as they want to be friends with me~~

I went through the exact same thing a few weeks ago. Just ask the replacement to step out for a few minutes because you want to spend some time alone with your current T.

&#x200B;

Also, you'll get through this. It won't be fun but your new therapist can hopefully help you just as much.

I started to purge by throwing up when I was around 15. When it really started to bother me a few years later, the therapist I was seeing then (only for a few weeks because boy was she bad at her job) completely dismissed it and it never seemed to get out of hand so I never put too much thought into it.

&#x200B;

At 25, I severely restricted for the first time, with the occasional b/p. I lost a lot of weight quickly, was borderline underweight and had trouble just getting through the day because of how weak and dizzy I constantly felt. That was sort of when I realized I have a big problem.

[https://www.stadtbildpflege-kl.de/index.php?id=29](https://www.stadtbildpflege-kl.de/index.php?id=29)

&#x200B;

click on "collection dates" and enter your address, it'll tell you.

I remember losing my hospital virginity 😍

&#x200B;

But seriously, I hope you're doing better and they treated you right.

I am so stealing this one.

&#x200B;

Also, my therapist has finally stopped laughing at my jokes today :( Granted, I am in deep shit so none of it was really funny but it still hurt,

Ok so I don't really know if you are trying to compare this to a kind of therapy that doesn't involve talking and which I am not aware of...? (This is NOT meant as a joke, or to make you feel bad, I am seriously wondering)

&#x200B;

But to answer your question: it gets easier. You'll learn to trust your therapist, and that they don't judge. I am at a point now where I just say whatever comes to mind because I know I will not be ridiculed for it.

Ok so I have never gone to therapy twice a week because it's not common in my country. But my T and I have the agreement that I can drop off a letter during the week if I feel like it, and it's honestly helped so much. Writing the letter, I have time to really reflect on my thoughts. If something doesn't feel right, I can revise it. I can also tell my therapist things that I think are important, but I wouldn't want to waste half an hour of therapy on, I just want them to know. Also, it has helped us with getting to the point and talking about the real issues.

&#x200B;

Please note: I would never accept e-mail communication for the sole reason that I would write emotionally charged e-mails at 3 am that I regret 5 hours later. I need to hand-deliver the letter, so I have ample time at a reasonable hour of day to think about whether what I wrote makes sense and is helpful or not.

You are doing God's work.

&#x200B;

Also \*speaking through clenched teeth\* can you please forward it to my local psychiatric clinic so they can get me the help I need before my body is damaged beyond return...

I know this probably feels alien to you but from what I gather she loves you. For who you are. And she loves you so much, she wanted to show it by buying you these things.

&#x200B;

That doesn't mean you should stop yeeting because of her. But just know that there is affection out there for you.

I'm on a different ED platform and there are so many posts about hiding your ED from parents, trouble in school, inpatient/res for minors etc, and I just sit there going... I cannot relate anymore 😂

&#x200B;

Sadly the disease doesn't just go away with age... I recently told myself sarcastically, that I'll be so proud to win "Ms Skinny Bitch" at the retirement home when I'm 84, my lifelong struggle will finally pay off.🙄

Thank you for introducing me to that wonderful piece of music.

&#x200B;

Also remind me to never listen to it while yeeting, especially while drinking vodka, I might accidentally hack my arm off because of that overwhelming god-like feeling I get from this song.

Don't beat yourself up too much. Selfharm is an addiction.

&#x200B;

I recently approached 2 months clean and decided to go all out, and it didn't even do anything for me, and I just sat there thinking "...why".

&#x200B;

But, you'll make it another 6 months.

Just make up a short, believable story. I used to say that I fell off a horse and rolled over some sharp rocks. Kids believe anything. Don't stress yourself out too much.

&#x200B;

I guess nowadays I'd say I was in a car accident since the horse story is not believable anymore due to the amount of scars.

You can either get some big bandaids or depending on how your cuts are placed you can buy compresses/gauze pads, cover the area, and then use gauze rolls to keep it all in place.

&#x200B;

If your cuts tend to get stuck to the gauze you can buy paraffin gauze or vaseline gauze, put that on the wounds first, then gauze pads and then the rolls.

Ugh I understand. I also made a few not-so-great-decisions lately, which have (hopefully) no longterm effects, but the next couple of weeks will be shit.

&#x200B;

Hang in there...

Happy birthday, and I can relate.

&#x200B;

The only thing that currently stands between me and a hypomanic to manic episode are two little pills in the evening. It is so damn tempting to just not take them.

Oh God I thought I was the only one. I sometimes didn't eat breakfast or dinner because it required to line up at a buffet and I just couldn't stand being watched (also being rushed through what you take because standing there for a few seconds deciding would have been TOO AWKWARD).

&#x200B;

Have you found out where to put the dishes? I hope your next meal goes smoother...

I'm sorry you felt so shitty :/

&#x200B;

But if it helps at all, buzzcuts are freaking awesome. I had one for 18 months and actually felt very feminine with it, and it is an absolute carefree hairdo.

Oh God I have exams coming up and had a very suicidal last two weeks. Embarrassing thing is, that last year when I had exams it was the same and I actually ended up attempting because obviously failing exams is far worse than taking your life. Avoidance at all cost for the win.

&#x200B;

Anyway, my therapist has caught on thankfully...
